tanjiro as the embodiment of kindness  even in battle is a very unique and  refreshing take on the shonen hero trope  that is worth looking at in detail i've  really been looking forward to talking  about tanzido for a while now now even  though i criticized the character  writing overall in my video on why you  should watch demon slayer tanjiro is not  only an exception to this but also  easily one of the most interesting ones  in the show  [Music]  in a weird way he is your very  stereotypical shonen hero on the one  side and yet something entirely new and  different on the other and i find his  personality and philosophy extremely  fascinating hi my name is manu the only  demons i've ever slain are the  ridiculously large mosquitoes here in  japan like i absolutely hate killing any  living thing but for mosquitoes i can go  full berserk mode those are the true  demons where was i oh yeah i've been  absolutely obsessed with the beauty of  the kimitsu nuyba anime and so i just  had no other choice but analyzed its  main character and found some  surprisingly deep writing behind it what  do i mean by that well on the surface  tanjiro kamado fits the typical shonen  hero mold pretty pretty nicely he's a  young boy check who goes on an adventure  fighting bad guys check trying to get  stronger to reach his goal check and has  a hidden power he didn't know about all  along check now tanjido isn't from a  noble family but if you've ever dreamed  of being a lord or lady i might have  just the thing for you this video is  sponsored by established titles a really  great project with the goal to preserve  the beautiful woodlands and nature of  scotland by selling a tiny piece of land  to you because based on a historic  scottish custom where land owners are  referred to as a lord or a lady you can  then use this title for yourself even on  official documents that allow for it in  return in cooperation with the global  reforestation charities one tree planted  and trees for the future they commit to  planting a tree with every single order  and by the way it's also a great last  minute gift i think if you're still  looking for christmas presents as you  can see i got mine so i'm now officially  lord o'hara and you can even see where  your land is exactly located online they  actually have a big holiday sale going  on right now with up to 80 off and using  my code you can get an additional 10 off  link in the description below now  tanjido's desire his big goal is super  clearly defined and tells us a lot about  his character after his entire family is  murdered except for his sister who is  turned into a demon tanjiro doesn't seek  revenge but simply wants to save the  last remaining member of his family and  turn nasuko back into a human and this  perfectly sets up the most important  character trait overall his empathy and  i found this super super interesting  because tanjiro is kind or at least  empathetic even to the demons that he  encounters  he is the only one that is willing to  see them as people but at the same time  he still wants to turn nasiku back at  first i found that a little bit  paradoxical but when you actually think  about it it speaks of a certain maturity  he's able to see them as people who were  turned into demons but he still dislikes  the condition of being a demon for  obvious reasons i mean they eat people  and [ __ ] now usually for most heroes  beneath that superficial desire that  they have there is actually a  subconscious need that a character has  some sort of lie that they believe about  the world that needs to be challenged  now at the beginning of the series it  would be very easy to say that tanjido's  need is to lose his nativity you simply  can't emphasize with everyone especially  not if they're trying to kill you and so  to me at the start it seemed like his  kindness and hate for violence might be  a real hindrance in his fight against  the most evil and strongest beings in  this world however i got proven wrong  relatively quickly tanjiro proves to us  very early on that he can do both be  kind and caring and do what is necessary  at the same time he has a sort of eerie  but kind ruthlessness in him that result  in scenes like this  [Music]  the point that i found really  astonishing to be honest is that tanjiro  doesn't really have a need to change he  is an incredibly kind and caring person  with a strong will to fight and get  stronger to protect his sister and those  who need protection and he simply stays  that person and as a result tanjido has  close to no character development in the  story now just like me you probably know  a few people who proclaim that if a  character has no character development  it's a bad character  which is just not true a character is  bad if he is inconsistent or simply  straight out boring the reason tanjido  stands out as a shown in hiro with this  however is that most chonin protagonists  have a positive character arc they start  with a relatively obvious fall to the  character that they resolve at the end  of the story  foreign  [Applause]  and so since it's a positive character  arc they become better people as the  story goes on heck even a all-powerful  character like saitama and one punch man  needs to learn that true happiness  doesn't come from fighting strong and  strong opponents but from the  relationships that he has built a very  rare but really interesting variation on  this is a negative character arc where  the hero becomes this illusion with the  world and becomes a worse person like  for example gone in hanzo  on the other hand has a flat character  arc yep that is a real thing he already  has accepted the world as it is and has  chosen his path of how to approach it in  his case kindness  now flat arcs are very very rare in  shonen for a reason because the audience  expects the hero to grow and change as  they face harder and harder obstacles  and so as a result flat character arcs  can be really confusing or frustrating  to people a really great example of this  i think is ichigo from bleach he also  barely changes throughout the story and  only gets stronger physically the  problem is is that bleach is way too  long for such a hero and so it starts  becoming dull at some point which was  ultimately its downfall demon slayer on  the other hand is relatively short the  manga is already over and there might be  even less than 100 anime episodes at the  end of it which allows us to focus on  the world and people around tanjiro  without it getting all too boring that  being said flat arcs really depend on  your taste and if you prefer seeing  someone grow as a person then that's  fair i actually totally get that but  that doesn't automatically make it bad  writing in tonjiro's case his only flaw  is his physical weakness that he has to  continually fight and struggle against  he hates how useless he is and how he  cannot help the people he wants to help  now one big criticism i personally have  here is that i feel like the characters  around tanjuro could use way stronger  character arcs i genuinely think that  tanjido as a pillar of goodness would  have actually been some really great  writing if the people around him had  changed a lot more in his presence than  they do in the end shinobu and kanao are  the closest that we get but it could  have been a lot more impactful i  personally think the people who change  the most are the demons and that only  after being defeated which i find a bit  of a shame so what is tanzidor all about  then well as most heroes he is the foil  and exact opposite to the villain of the  story muzan in every aspect  everything from the motivations to the  ideals and values that there represents  while muzan only cares about himself and  sees everyone around him demons and  humans alike as tools to use for his  pleasure and desires tanjiro's central  defining trait is his altruism his  greatest priority will always be the  lives of the people around him and so  even though he's fighting against  literal demons he sees every last of  them as people and so while he doesn't  necessarily forgive and still condemns  them for their actions in the end he  acknowledges that all of them were human  beings at one point and tries his best  to respect their feelings even though he  has to take their lives in order to  protect his own or that of his friends  he tries to give them the kindness that  they were denied during their lifetime  giving them a bit of redemption and a  final chance to reflect on their lives  and so the challenge of his entire arc  is purely about finding strength and  power without throwing away his kindness  or his humanity instead of changing and  becoming a better or worse person  tanjiro is all about staying the great  man he already is he strongly embodies  the ideal of existentialism which is  basically the idea that his life derives  meaning and happiness from selflessly  giving to the people around him and  that's the point where he even tries to  give meaning to everyone that he meets  now even though the first season of  demon slayer was an absolute firework in  terms of production for me as i  explained in my previous video the mugen  train movie really took me by surprise  with its storyline especially for  tanjido the fact that we got to  physically enter everyone's deepest  desire and even more interestingly see  everyone's subconsciousness was an  incredibly creative choice in my opinion  i doubt that this was done on purpose  but the visual nature of the anime makes  up for a lot of the character writing in  my opinion and so literally visualizing  everyone's personality was a really  creative and awesome move touche i  wonder what my subconsciousness will  look like mostly coffee probably and you  gently hitting the subscribe button  anyways in tanjiro's dream instead of  returning back to his family left dead  after being massacred he finds them  alive and well instead well at first at  least  yes  [Laughter]  tanjido's desire is very simple to just  live a happy life with his family after  all he's just a simple charcoal burner  and a simple elder brother who became  the breadwinner at a young age after the  death of his father and so this dream  brings to light all of his trauma  regrets and guilt that he must have  carried with him at all times as he hugs  his siblings tightly apologizing over  and over again even though his dream  self doesn't know exactly why he was  apologizing and crying tanjito  understandably has a pretty severe case  of survivor's guilt as he was the only  one who got away unharmed especially as  the oldest brother  [Laughter]  it's really the first time since going  on his journey where he can pause and  reflect on that fact because he kept  himself distracted with his mission to  get stronger and at least save nizuko  but at the same time it once again shows  us that tanjiro is already mature and  prioritizes those around him instead of  allowing himself to be drawn in by the  thing that he wants the most he's able  to let go of the past in order to save  the people on board of the train hell he  even goes so far as to kill himself in  order to wake up which is pretty radical  if i'm being honest and yet despite all  the pain and regret inside him when we  get to enter tanjido's subconsciousness  it still is an endless oasis of love and  warmth with his core shining as bright  as the sun the thing that demons fear  the most even after all the traumatic  events he already had to live through he  still remains unconditionally kind to  the point where you could call him the  personification of kindness himself  the boy who entered this place was  reduced to tears and is healed from his  darkness exactly the thing i would have  liked to see for more characters around  him tanjiro is the son that frees people  and demons alike from the darkness of  their hearts and their eternal pain and  to me that's what makes him such a  unique character in the shonen genre

